<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Karagwe</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1963 CE (approx.) · capital Bweranyange</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom west of Lake Victoria whose ruler Rumanyika welcomed the explorers Speke and Grant on their way to the source of the Nile.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Karagwe emerged around the 15th or 16th century, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinda-dynasty">Hinda dynasty</a> that also ruled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-ankole">Ankole</a> and other states of the region. It lay in the hills of north-western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagera-river">Kagera river</a>, and traced its royal line to the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhinda">Ruhinda</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The mukama (king) ruled with chiefs over pastoral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahima">Bahima</a> and farming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bairu">Bairu</a>. Karagwe grew rich in the 19th century as a staging post on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> linking <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Buganda</a> with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Zanzibar</a> traders, trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> for cloth and firearms. King Rumanyika I hosted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">John Hanning Speke</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-augustus-grant">James Grant</a> in 1861, and their accounts made Karagwe known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Succession wars and disease weakened the kingdom in the late 19th century, and it was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> and then <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tanganyika-territory">Tanganyika Territory</a> as a native authority. The chiefdom was abolished by the government of independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tanganyika">Tanganyika</a> in 1963.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1500 CE – 1963 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Horn and East Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:9.21%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1963 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bweranyange</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ndagara I, Rumanyika I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Runyambo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tanzania">Tanzania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karagwe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1732&amp;focus=kingdom-of-karagwe" aria-label="Kingdom of Karagwe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5537 2244 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Karagwe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5857 2500L6087 2688M5857 2500L6087 2688"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6087 2688h0M6087 2688h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5857 2500h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5857 2500h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bweranyange. <a href="/atlas?year=1732&amp;focus=kingdom-of-karagwe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

